    "name": "Chapleau Cree First Nation",
    "section": "First 10 (Pilot)",
    "link": "https://chapleaucree.ca/",
    "contact": "Healthcare Coordinator        \nMarlene Kapashesit        \n\n705-864-0200 Ext. 202\n\nContact Us page",
    "physical": "The CCHC Health Centre provides health awareness, education and prevention workshops and seminars, an exercise program, and a doctor available one day per month through Family Health Team. It also describes supports for older adults and adults with disabilities to help them remain independent in their own homes as long as possible.",
    "mental": "Specific mental-health-related community programming is visible in weekly community pamphlets through 1:1 sessions, sharing circles, storytelling & sharing, and Addictions Awareness Dinner hosted by Chapleau Cree Health Services / Life’s Sacred Journey.\nNewsletter\nNewsletter",
    "spiritual": "Through programming and services, the community uses a Two-Eyed seeing approach with a holistic approach to health and wellness. \nServices and programming offered regarding physical and mental/emotional health embed spiritual teachings. Taykwa Tagamou Nation ensures that all services come from a holistic approach to health and well-being.\n\nMushkegowuk Health \n\nSpiritual support and services include:\n\nSupportive Traditional Practitioners, Healers and Counsellors who provide traditional healing, counselling, cultural events, and spiritual services to the Indigenous peoples within the Mushkegowuk region\nPromotion of culture, traditional methodology, health, and spiritual maintenance for a healthier lifestyle and to prevent illnesses\nAdvocacy on behalf of Mushkegowuk community members regarding equitable access to traditional healing, cultural, and health services \nTraditional ceremonies, teachings and medicines\n\nReference\nhttps://www.mushkegowukhealth.com/traditional-healing-program",
    "emotional": "Programs includes sharing circles, storytelling & sharing, 1:1 sessions, and wellness activities coordinated through Health and Life's Sacred Journey.\nWeekly-Pamphlate-March-23-29-2029",
    "survivors": "There is no specific programming for survivors within the community but at a political territorial level, there is support as well as with CSSA. Nishnawbe Aski Nation created an advisory council named The SAFE council which stands for Survivor and Family Empowerment. It is a survivor-led initiative that promotes the inclusion of survivors’ and families’ voices and provides direction for the vision of how the work by the department needs to be approached. The group utilizes their knowledge, lived experience, and expertise as survivors of residential school.",
    "youth": "The overall support within the community is vague on the community’s website however there is a newsletter that provides information about youth workshops and mental health supports. \n\n\nYouth are supported through community programming, education services, and youth-focused wellness programs including Youth Self Defence\nNewsletter",
    "connect": "Use of cultural crafts and ceremonies would be beneficial. Also use of arts based activities would be great as well. It is important to hear from the youth in the community to hear how they want to be connected to the survivors in the community.",
    "population": "898",
    "populationNote": "Registered Population as of March, 2026 - 898\nGovernment of Canada  \n\npopulation gathered from Government of Canada as of October 2025\nregistered population - 866\nRegistered Population - Government of Canada\n\npopulation information from Wikipedia source as of October 2019\ntotal population - 498 \n(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapleau_Cree_First_Nation)",
