        "communityKey": "catlakefn",
        "item": "How are youth supported within the community?",
        "description": "Previous information:\n-Choose Life Program:Offers land-based activities and programming promoting mental, emotional, and behavioral health for ages 0-18, funded by Nishnawbe-Aski Nation (NAN).\nFamily Well Being/Hurt & Healing Programs: Provide culturally-safe, art-based mental health support, addressing trauma, addiction, and legacy of residential schools through counselling, family circles, and traditional healing.\nOshkeemekana Treatment Centre: A residential facility for NAN youth (12-15) with addictions, offering clinical care, life skills, counseling, and traditional land-based healing.\nBrighter Futures: Runs a youth drop-in center, hosts sports, and teaches healthy living. \nCat Lake First Nation - Oshkeemekana Residential Treatment Centre - northwesthealthline.ca\nhttps://ocaarchives.files.wordpress.com/2019/01/foh-report.pdf\n\nRevised information:\nCat Lake First Nation supports youth through Choose Life, Jordan’s Principle, Lawrence Wesley Education Centre, Oshkeemekana Pimogeekawin, Nodin Mental Health, and Brighter Futures. Choose Life offers predominantly land-based activities for children and youth ages 0–18 and promotes mental, emotional, and behavioural well-being. Jordan’s Principle supports children who need help right away and helps families request products, services, and supports. Lawrence Wesley Education Centre serves Junior Kindergarten to Grade 8 and has capacity for over 150 students. Oshkeemekana Pimogeekawin focuses on protecting children, keeping children within their families, and building strong families. Secondary directory information also identifies Brighter Futures programming, including a youth drop-in centre, healthy-living education, activities, and sports events.",
        "date": "2026-06-10 11:08:46.640000",
