        "communityKey": "catlakefn",
        "item": "How can we connect survivors and youth?",
        "description": "Previous information:\nPrograms like \"Choose Life\", and traditional activities (snaring, camping) connect youth with Elders and the land for holistic well-being, contrasting with institutional settings.\nCHOOSE LIFE | Cat Lake First Nation | ᐱᔑᐎᓴᑲᐃ ᑲᓂᒃ\n\nRevised information:\nCat Lake First Nation can connect Elders and youth through land-based Choose Life activities, Oshkeemekana Pimogeekawin’s traditional values/family-strengthening approach, and Elder knowledge-sharing around Anishinaabemowin, culture, and ancestral legacy. The official website identifies Elders as living archives who safeguard and impart language, culture, and ancestral legacy, while Choose Life provides land-based activities for children and youth ages 0–18. Oshkeemekana Pimogeekawin also emphasizes cherishing traditional values and drawing strength from families. These sources support intergenerational connection through land-based activities, language, cultural teachings, traditional values, family connection, and Elder guidance.",
