    "name": "Fort William, Of Lake Superior",
    "section": "North",
    "link": "Fort William First Nation",
    "contact": "Melissa Zeleny – Interim Executive Assistant\nPhone:  807.623.9543 | Ext. 806\nEmail: adminofficer@fwfn.com\n\nHealth Department\nSierra Bannon - Health Reception\nPhone: (807) 622-8802\nEmail: healthreception@fwfn.com\n\nLuanne Maki - Health Manager\nPhone: (807) 623-9543 | Ext. 209\nluanne@fwfn.com\n\nSheri Boucher - Director of Community Wellbeing\nPhone: (807) 623-9543 | Ext. 809\nsheriboucher@fwfn.com\n\nEducation Department\nBrittany Anness - Education Manager\nPhone: (807) 623-9543 | Ext. 205\nEmail: brittanyanness@fwfn.com\n\nShyra Bannon - Education Coordinator\nPhone: (807) 623-9543 | Ext. 270\nEmail: PostSecondary@fwfn.com\n\nMarie Solomon - Education Data Entry Clerk\nPhone: (807) 623-9543 | Ext. 230\nEmail: mariesolomon@fwfn.com\n\nEmployee Directory",
    "physical": "Physical health support is provided through FWFN’s Health Services Department, Health Centre, Diabetes Education, Family Centre – Early Years Program, and medical transportation supports. The Health Centre says family physicians, nurse practitioners, nurses, and a registered social worker are accepting new patient applications for expectant mothers, students, and people with chronic illness, and the Medical Van provides transportation within Thunder Bay for appointments with general practitioners, specialists, therapy referrals, dental, and optometry. Diabetes Education is supported by an Aboriginal Diabetes Coordinator, and the Family Centre lists Parents & Tots programming. These services help fill gaps in local primary care access, chronic illness support, diabetes education, early-years family support, and transportation to health appointments.",
    "mental": "Mental health programming is provided through FWFN’s Addictions & Wellness / Mental Health services. The program provides support to individuals and family members affected by mental health concerns in a holistic, culturally sensitive, non-biased, safe, and confidential way. It includes education, advocacy, referral support, counselling and treatment referrals, service planning, wellness support, community programs, workshops, presentations, harm reduction outreach, lunch-and-learns, and youth engagement through monthly youth programming and culturally based prevention/addiction education.",
    "spiritual": "Broader spiritual and cultural support is reflected through FWFN’s mission to support physical, mental, emotional, and spiritual wellbeing and through the Youth & Social Development Cultural Department, which teaches, promotes, preserves, and enhances Ojibway language and Anishinaabe culture through media, cultural classes and activities, traditional stories, immersion experiences, and other programming. FWFN also lists cultural events such as the Summer Powwow and Traditional Gathering, which create spaces for tradition, reconnection, reflection, education, and healing.",
    "emotional": "Emotional support can be provided through FWFN’s broader Mental Health, Addictions & Wellness, Family Wellness, Family Support, Social Services, and Youth & Social Development supports. The Addictions & Wellness page describes safe and confidential services, advocacy, referral support, counselling and treatment referrals, service planning, wellness support, harm reduction outreach, and education on mental health and wellness. The employee directory also lists Family Wellness, Band Representative, Family Support, Family Well-Being, Social Services, and Mental Health & Addictions staff, showing that emotional and family-support pathways are available within the community structure.",
    "survivors": "FWFN has direct community support connected to Indian Residential School Survivors. The employee directory lists Liz Esquega as Indian Residential School Program Coordinator and an Indian Residential School Program Assistant position. FWFN’s Youth & Social Development also lists a Traditional Gathering, described as a time to honour the resilience, dignity, and strength of survivors, remember the children who never made it home, educate others about the ongoing impacts of colonial history, acknowledge the past, support healing, and foster understanding. Broader support is also available through FWFN’s mental health, addictions/wellness, family wellness, and cultural programming",
    "youth": "Youth are supported through FWFN’s Youth & Social Development Department, Youth Department, Recreation Department, Cultural Department, Education Department, and Family Centre – Early Years Program. The Youth Department provides a safe, accessible space for young people and offers social, cultural, recreation, and preventative services, while recreation programming supports physical, mental, and social wellbeing. Youth activities include Niibin Day Camp, Mountain Keeper Program, March Break programming, youth recreation, cultural activities, and special events. Education supports include JK–Grade 12 tuition agreements, school busing, elementary/secondary education, post-secondary education, driver’s education funding, graduation bursary awards, and travel bursaries for school-sanctioned excursions.",
    "connect": "Community possible have community-based pathways to connect Indian Residential School Survivors with youth through its Indian Residential School Program, Traditional Gathering, Cultural Department, Youth Department, Niibin Day Camp, Mountain Keeper Program, and cultural/recreational programming. The Traditional Gathering is specifically described as honouring survivors, remembering the children who never made it home, educating people about colonial history, supporting healing, and fostering understanding. Youth programming also includes cultural awareness, traditional stories, immersion experiences, land/culture learning, prevention programming, and activities that build relationships with resource people in the community. These official sources show strong pathways for connecting youth with Survivors/Elders through culture, history, healing, land-based learning, and community gatherings.",
    "population": "3097.0",
