    "name": "Lac Seul First Nation",
    "section": "North",
    "link": "https://lacseulfn.org",
    "contact": "Frenchman's Head Contact Info\nBand Office\nPhone: 807-582-3503 \nFax: 807-582-3449 \n\nClinic\nPhone: 807-582-3512\nFax: 807-582-3837\n\nEducation Authority\nPhone: 807-582-3499\nFax: 807-582-3431\n\nHealth Authority\nPhone: 807-582-3459\nFax: 807-582-3857\n\nKejick Bay Contact Info\nBand Office\nPhone: 807-582-3211\nFax: 807-582-3493\n\nClinic\nPhone: 807-582-9812\nFax: 807-582-9878\n\nWhitefish Bay Contact Info\nBand Office\nPhone: 807-582-3228\nFax: 807-582-3839\n\nClinic\nPhone: 807-582-3293\nFax: 807-582-3257\n\nContact Page",
    "physical": "Physical health support is provided through Lac Seul First Nation’s Health & Nursing services. It provides Telehealth, which allows clients to have virtual consultations with specialists; Dental and Vision, including dental visits every three months and optometry visits; Medical Transportation, which runs in all three communities to transport patients to appointments in Sioux Lookout and other urban areas and also delivers lab boxes and prescriptions; and the Suboxone Treatment Program, which addresses addiction issues in the community. These services help fill gaps in specialist access, dental/vision care, medical travel, prescription/lab delivery, and addiction-related health support.",
    "mental": "Mental health support is provided through Lac Seul’s Dehnawehmakanug Prevention Services and Health & Nursing programs. Prevention Services is designed to proactively address challenges and crises through education, early identification, practical skill-building, resilience-building, strengthening support networks, and promoting healthy behaviours. Listed programs include the Pizaaniziwin Program, which provides mental health and addictions counselling, case management, one-on-one support, connections to clinical supports, mental health assessments, and medication management; the Oshki-Maaidaa Biimadiziwin Aftercare Program, which includes activities, land-based programs, life skills, and training for people in recovery; and outreach support that includes visiting, peer support, basic necessities, transportation, and crisis follow-up.",
    "spiritual": "Broader spiritual, cultural, and healing support is shown through official Lac Seul programming such as the Bringing Our Children Home Initiative, which includes objectives around ceremony and healing, honouring children, public education on residential school legacy, and commemoration of Survivors. Prevention Services also includes land-based programs, traditional healing-related recovery supports, Elders and trained counsellors in gender-based violence recovery support, and wellness checks that connect members to community services.",
    "emotional": "Emotional support can be provided through Lac Seul’s Prevention Services, Pizaaniziwin Program, Pibaa Maadiziwin Outreach Support Program, and related recovery supports. The official Prevention page says the department proactively addresses challenges and crises by building awareness, resilience, support networks, and healthy behaviours. The Pizaaniziwin Program provides mental health and addictions counselling, case management, one-on-one support, clinical connections, mental health assessments, and medication management, while Pibaa Maadiziwin provides visiting, peer support, basic necessities, transportation, crisis follow-up, wellness checks, food hampers, care packages, harm-reduction supplies, and connections to community services.",
    "survivors": "Lac Seul First Nation has a clear survivor-related support pathway through the Bringing Our Children Home Initiative, which was created after the Kamloops Indian Residential School findings and focuses on the former Pelican Lake Indian Residential School, Zone Indian Hospital, and local and municipal cemeteries. Its objectives include uncovering the truth, bringing children home, honouring the children, ceremony and healing, public education on the legacy of residential schools, and memorialization/commemoration of Survivors. Methods include community engagement sessions, interviews with Survivors, newsletters and communication, research, story and historical record collection, ground searches, memorial design, and repatriating records to affected communities, with guidance from a Survivors Working Circle and Lac Seul Chief and Council",
    "youth": "Youth are supported through Lac Seul’s Youth Services & Community Recreation, Education, and Post Majority supports. Youth Services lists Choose Life, which supports First Nations in responding to and preventing youth suicide by strengthening protective factors, enhancing wellness, and ensuring young people have safe, supportive environments rooted in culture, identity, and community connection. It also lists Jordan’s Principle, which helps families access services, care, support, referrals, needs identification, and assessment coordination, as well as Youth Recreation, including support for a youth volleyball team. Education support includes Obishikokaang Elementary School, which provides learning opportunities that support, challenge, and inspire students, with a library, gym, daycare, industrial kitchen, and JK–Grade 8 classrooms. Post Majority supports youth and young adults ages 18–26 who have aged out of Child and Family Services with housing, education, employment, life skills, training, and transition into independence/adulthood.",
    "connect": "Lac Seul has a strong community-based pathway for connecting Survivors with youth through the Bringing Our Children Home Initiative, Survivors Working Circle, youth wellness programming, education, and culture-based supports. The Bringing Our Children Home Initiative includes Survivor interviews, community engagement sessions, story and historical record collection, public education on the legacy of residential schools, ceremony and healing, and commemoration of Survivors. Youth Services also supports safe and supportive environments rooted in culture, identity, and community connection through Choose Life, Jordan’s Principle, and youth recreation. Together, these official sources show possible ways to connect youth with Survivors through education, storytelling, ceremony/healing, community engagement, culture, and wellness programming",
