        "item": "How are survivors supported within the community?",
        "description": "Previous information:\nThe new Healing Lodge is being built to utilize aftercare activities and land-based activities to assist in the healing process for individuals struggling with addictions and trauma.\n\nRevised information:\nLac Seul First Nation has a clear survivor-related support pathway through the Bringing Our Children Home Initiative, which was created after the Kamloops Indian Residential School findings and focuses on the former Pelican Lake Indian Residential School, Zone Indian Hospital, and local and municipal cemeteries. Its objectives include uncovering the truth, bringing children home, honouring the children, ceremony and healing, public education on the legacy of residential schools, and memorialization/commemoration of Survivors. Methods include community engagement sessions, interviews with Survivors, newsletters and communication, research, story and historical record collection, ground searches, memorial design, and repatriating records to affected communities, with guidance from a Survivors Working Circle and Lac Seul Chief and Council",
