        "item": "How are youth supported within the community?",
        "description": "Previous information:\nAboriginal Headstart (early childhood), Family Well Being Program includes child/youth support groups and school programs.\n\nRevised information:\nYouth are supported through Lac Seul’s Youth Services & Community Recreation, Education, and Post Majority supports. Youth Services lists Choose Life, which supports First Nations in responding to and preventing youth suicide by strengthening protective factors, enhancing wellness, and ensuring young people have safe, supportive environments rooted in culture, identity, and community connection. It also lists Jordan’s Principle, which helps families access services, care, support, referrals, needs identification, and assessment coordination, as well as Youth Recreation, including support for a youth volleyball team. Education support includes Obishikokaang Elementary School, which provides learning opportunities that support, challenge, and inspire students, with a library, gym, daycare, industrial kitchen, and JK–Grade 8 classrooms. Post Majority supports youth and young adults ages 18–26 who have aged out of Child and Family Services with housing, education, employment, life skills, training, and transition into independence/adulthood.",
