    "name": "Wawakapewin First Nation",
    "section": "North",
    "link": "http://www.wawakapewin.ca/",
    "contact": "Chief Sheila Childsforever - Chief of Wawakapewin First Nation\nEmai: sheilac@wawakapewin.ca\n\nJoshua Frogg - Band Manager\nEmail: joshuaf@wawakapewin.ca\n\nBerry Frogg - Ecomic Development Officer\nEmail: berryf@wawakapewin.ca\n\nNoreen Meekis - Health Coordinator \nEmail: noreenm@wawakapewin.ca\n\nMelissa Childforever - Band Administrator\nEmail: melissac@wawakapewin.ca\n\nWawakapewin First Nation",
    "physical": "Physical health support is accessed through the Kasabonika Lake Nursing Station, 37 km northeast of Wawakapewin, with health care from community health nurses, visiting physicians, and home-care workers. Services include health awareness/counselling, education and prevention workshops/clinics for child care, diabetes, elderly/chronic disease, immunization, sexual health and communicable diseases, treatment of ongoing health issues, emergency services, advocacy with health professionals, prenatal care coordination, medical-travel coordination, and OTN access for specialist consultations, tele-diagnostics, workshops, and reduced travel needs.",
    "mental": "Broader support can be provided through Health Services and the Family Well Being Program. Health Services includes health counselling, advocacy, referrals, and prevention education, while Family Well Being focuses on community wellbeing, healthy family relationships, reducing family violence, reducing youth justice involvement, and reducing children in care, with listed activities including counselling, child/youth support groups, alcohol and drug-abuse prevention/intervention, family circles, referrals, school programs, and land-based healing events.",
    "spiritual": "Broader cultural/spiritual support can be provided through the Family Well Being Program, which lists Native-language revitalization, community feasts, family circles, school programs, and traditional land-based healing events as program activities",
    "emotional": "Emotional support can be provided through Health Services and the Family Well Being Program. Supports include health counselling, advocacy, referrals, family circles, counselling, child/youth support groups, alcohol and drug-abuse prevention/intervention, school programs, community feasts, Native-language revitalization, and traditional land-based healing events, helping address family stress, emotional wellbeing, family violence prevention, youth support, addictions prevention, and culturally grounded healing.",
    "survivors": "Broader Elder and wellness support can be provided through Health Services and Family Well Being, including elderly/chronic-disease supports through the Kasabonika Lake Nursing Station, health counselling, referrals, advocacy, family circles, counselling, community feasts, Native-language revitalization, and traditional land-based healing events.",
    "youth": "Youth are supported through the Family Well Being Program and broader Health Services. Family Well Being lists child/youth support groups, school programs, alcohol and drug-abuse prevention/intervention, family circles, counselling, referrals, Native-language revitalization, community feasts, and traditional land-based healing events; Health Services also supports child-care-related prevention workshops/clinics, health counselling, referrals, advocacy, and access to medical care through the Kasabonika Lake Nursing Station.",
    "connect": "Broader connection can be supported through the Family Well Being Program’s family circles, child/youth support groups, school programs, Native-language revitalization, community feasts, and traditional land-based healing events, creating possible community-based spaces for youth to connect with Elders/knowledge holders",
