    "name": "Wunnumin First Nation",
    "section": "North",
    "link": "https://wunnumin.com/",
    "contact": "Joyce Angees - Band Administrator\nPhone: 807-442-2559 | EXT 1021\nEmail: joycea@wunnumin.ca\n\nRachel Mamakawa - Health Director\nPhone: 807-442-2573 | EXT 218\nEmail: rachelma@wunnumin.ca",
    "physical": "Physical health support is provided through Wanapetum Memorial Health Centre, which is for Diabetes Program, Health Services, Healthy Babies Healthy Children, Home and Community Care, Home and Community Care Physiotherapy Services, and NNADAP. Nursing Station provides basic medical and health care that promotes physical and mental health, while Home and Community Care supports ill, injured, or elderly members in their homes, and in-home therapy may include physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech-language pathology, and case management.",
    "mental": "Mental health support is listed through Wanapetum Memorial Health Centre, Health Services, NNADAP, and the Family Well Being Program. The Nursing Station promotes physical and mental health, and Family Well Being activities include family circles, counselling, child/youth support groups, referrals, school programs, alcohol and drug-abuse prevention/intervention, community feasts, Native-language revitalization, and traditional land-based healing events.",
    "spiritual": "Broader cultural/spiritual support can be provided through the Family Well Being Program, which lists Native-language revitalization, community feasts, family circles, school programs, and traditional land-based healing events; the community website also includes community story/history content connected to Wunnumin’s name and place.",
    "emotional": "Emotional support can be provided through Health Services, NNADAP, Home and Community Care, and the Family Well Being Program. Listed supports include counselling, child/youth support groups, family circles, referrals, school programs, alcohol and drug-abuse prevention/intervention, community feasts, Native-language revitalization, and traditional land-based healing events, along with home-based support for ill, injured, or elderly members.",
    "survivors": "Family Well-Being Programs: These programs provide family circles, counselling, and child/youth support groups.\n\nCrisis Prevention and Response: Immediate on-site or telephone assistance is available. This includes debriefing and stabilization after a crisis, as well as practical help like meal preparation and transportation for families in distress.",
    "youth": "Youth are supported through Lydia Lois Beardy Memorial School, Education Authority, Day Care Centre, Family Well Being, Healthy Babies Healthy Children, and health/wellness supports. The school provides JK–Grade 10 education with Native Language and Native Studies, gym, library, computer lab, breakfast/lunch through the Youth Café, and OSSLT participation; Education Authority supports elementary/secondary students and administers the school; Day Care provides toddler and preschool programs; Family Well Being includes child/youth support groups, school programs, language revitalization, community feasts, and land-based healing events.",
    "connect": "Broader connection can be supported through Family Well Being, Lydia Lois Beardy Memorial School, Native Language and Native Studies, school programs, community feasts, family circles, child/youth support groups, Native-language revitalization, traditional land-based healing events, and Wunnumin’s community story/place-based history, creating possible spaces for youth to connect with Elders/knowledge holders.",
    "population": "714.0",
