        "item": "How are youth supported within the community?",
        "description": "Previous information:\nResearch Link sources:\nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre \n\nEnji-maawinjidiyaang Indigenous Friendship Centre\n\n\nWasa-Nabin Program (Ages 13-18): A self-development program focusing on leadership, goal setting, and healthy lifestyle choices for urban Indigenous youth, providing one-to-one support and group activities.\n\nCultural & Wellness Activities: Youth engage in cultural camps, workshops, and traditional activities to build identity, connection to elders, and mental wellness.\n\nSupport Services: Offers advocacy, justice services, and mentorship to help youth navigate various challenges, with a focus on prevention and community-based alternatives.\n\nEducation & Employment: Programs such as the Alternative Secondary School Program and Apatisiwin assist in academic and career development.\n\nRevised information:\nWasa-Nabin Program (ages 13-18: leadership, goal-setting, healthy lifestyle, one-to-one support and group activities); cultural camps and workshops; advocacy and justice services; Alternative Secondary School Program and Apatisiwin (academic/career development).\nLink:\nhttps://www.enjimaawinjidiyaang.com/",
