    "physical": "NEONATAL SERVICES\nPre/post-natal support for high-risk mothers and infants.\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/neonatal-services\n\nFAMILY WELL-BEING\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/family-well-being",
    "mental": "ADULT MENTAL HEALTH (Mino Madzwin):              As per website they provide voluntary, confidential and comprehensive mental wellness supports for members of the seven First Nations along the North Shore, on and off reserve.\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/adult-mental-health\n\nCHILDREN'S MENTAL HEALTH\nprovide voluntary, confidential and comprehensive mental wellness supports to children and youth 4-24 years of age as well as to parents and care-givers.\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/childrens-mental-health",
    "spiritual": "CULTURAL SERVICES\nAs per website The Elder’s Council continues to provide a tremendous amount of knowledge that helps ensure that Nogdawindamin provides culturally appropriate services to all the children and families served. \nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/cultural-services",
    "emotional": "FAMILY WELL-BEING\nFamily Well-Being is an intensive early intervention program focused on providing support to families to minimize identified risk of involvement in Child Welfare, deescalating protection concerns, and supporting the reunification of families with children in out of home placements\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/family-well-being",
    "survivors": "ALTERNATIVE CARE (Genawenman Binojiuk)\nCommunity-based services grounded in Anishnawbek values to keep children in communityThe goal of Alternative Care is to work with the Nogdawindamin team to help the child and family reunite. In Anishnawbek tradition, it has been the duty of aunties, uncles, grandparents, sisters and brothers to assist as temporary care givers. \nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/AC",
    "youth": "YOUTH PREVENTION SERVICES\nMino Madzwin, Education Liaison and Youth in Transition programs.\nLink:\nhttps://www.nog.ca/services/youth-prevention-services",
