    "link": "https://www.kgcfs.org/",
    "contact": "MAIN OFFICE (per kgcfs.org):\n Main Office\n 98 Pottawatomi Ave\n Wikwemikong, ON\n P0P 2J0\n Tel: 705-370-2100\n Fax: 705-859-2195\n Toll-Free: 1-800-268-1899 Foster Care / Alternative Care Department Restricted Access\n 36 Tower Street,\n Wikwemikong, ON\n P0P 2J0 Admin. / IT. / Finance Department\n Restricted Access\n 15 Edward Street Unit #6\n Wikwemikong, ON\n P0P 2J0 Newgate Office\n 866 Newgate Ave. Unit #1\n Sudbury, ON\n P3A 5J9 Enji-maajiigawat Mina-waadiziwin\n Restricted Access\n 3260 Bancroft Drive\n Sudbury, ON\n P3B 1V3\n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/contact/",
    "physical": "SPECIALIZED SERVICES: The programs do touch on physical health as part of holistic well-being. Specifically, the Youth-in-Transition program helps youth build personal plans that include physical well-being, and the Housing Support Program helps youth navigate and connect to external health and mental health services.\n \n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/specialized-services/",
    "mental": "FAMILY COUNSELLOR PROGRAM:\n Offers intensive, short-term crisis intervention and family education services to address child neglect, abuse, or imminent risk of family breakdown.\n \n AAZ-GAA-BWI-TAAD-WIN (FAMILY TEAM GATHERING):\n Brings families and service providers together to develop plans that explicitly address child welfare concerns, including underlying determinants of addictions and mental health concerns.\n \n YOUTH-IN-TRANSITION:\n Ensures youth have a balanced personal well-being plan that actively incorporates mental health, alongside emotional, spiritual, and physical well-being.\n \n HOUSING SUPPORT PROGRAM:\n Connects transitioning youth to existing community systems and resources, including dedicated health and mental health services..\n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/specialized-services/",
    "spiritual": "BICULTURAL PRACTICE AND SERVICE:\n Allows service users to access traditional cultural and spiritual practices, including healing sessions with traditional counselors, gatherings, lodges, or ceremonies based on the family's chosen spiritual path.\n \n TRADITIONAL RESOURCES & ADVISORS:\n Provides direct access to Traditional/Spiritual Advisors to assist with wholistic wellness and healing, including Elders, Traditional Practitioners, Knowledge Keepers, Pipe Carriers, and Lodge Keepers.\n \n CULTURAL DAY EVENTS & CEREMONIES:\n Coordinates required ceremonies and bi-annual Cultural Day Events that provide a safe environment for individuals seeking help, traditional teachings, and guidance toward a path of spiritual healing.\n \n MID-WINTER ROUND DANCE SOCIAL:\n An annual cultural event that allows families to gather in a healthy manner to grieve, celebrate life with song and dance, and remember loved ones who have passed onto the spirit world..\n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/cultural-services/",
    "emotional": "YOUTH-IN-TRANSITION:\n Provides direct support to enhance life and social skills, build motivation and self-esteem, and help youth navigate peer pressures. Incorporates dedicated resources to ensure youth establish a balanced well-being plan that actively targets emotional wellness.\n \n FAMILY COUNSELLOR PROGRAM:\n Delivers intensive, short-term crisis intervention and emotional coping skills to families facing imminent risk of breakdown, reinforcing positive and responsive parenting dynamics.\n \n FAMILY FINDING PROGRAM:\n Focuses on reconnecting youth disconnected from their community to build permanent kinship networks, resulting in an improved sense of belonging and overall emotional well-being. link:https://www.kgcfs.org/cultural-services/",
    "survivors": "CUSTOMARY CARE & FOSTER CARE\n Administered locally through individual communities; recruits First Nations foster homes to keep children connected to roots, culture and language.\n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/cultural-services/ https://www.kgcfs.org/customary-care-participants/ https://www.kgcfs.org/become-a-foster-parent/",
    "youth": "YOUTH-IN-TRANSITION PROGRAM:\n Serves Anishinabek youth ages 16 to 24 by providing life and social skills training, independent living support, financial literacy, education planning, and assistance finding employment.\n \n HOUSING SUPPORT PROGRAM:\n Provides culturally appropriate housing aid to youth ages 16 and 17 to help them find and sustain long-term housing, build independent living skills, and execute personal housing goals.\n \n EDUCATION LIAISON PROGRAM:\n Assists eligible youth from Junior Kindergarten up to Grade 12 with culturally responsive school system navigation, resolving learning barriers, and promoting academic achievement. Link:https://www.kgcfs.org/specialized-services/",
    "connect": "CULTURAL DEPARTMENT\n Cultural ceremonies and gatherings (e.g. Annual Nibi and Letting Go Ceremony) bringing community together.\n Link:\n https://www.kgcfs.org/cultural-services/",
