    "name": "Ontario Aboriginal Housing (Sault Ste. Marie)",
    "section": "Child and Family Services",
    "link": "https://www.ontarioaboriginalhousing.ca/contact",
    "contact": "Contact Us • Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services \nfacebook link : Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services | Sault Sainte Marie ON \nOntario Aboriginal Housing Support Services Corporation | Property Management and Development - Sault Ste. Marie Chamber of Commerce, ON    \nProvincial Head Office (Sault Ste. Marie):\n\n\n• Phone: 705-256-1876\n\n\n• Toll Free: 1-866-391-1061\n\n\n• Address: 500 Bay St, Sault Ste. Marie, ON P6A 1X5\n\n\n• https://www.ontarioaboriginalhousing.ca/\n\n\n\nRegional Offices:\n\n\n• Sudbury/North: (Managed via SSM/Dryden)\n\n\n• Dryden: 24 Duke St, P8N 0A3\n\n\n• Peterborough: 721 Monaghan Rd, K9J 0J7\n\n\n\nEmail:\n\n\n• General: info@oahssc.ca\n\n\n• Programs: ishp@oahssc.ca",
    "physical": "Mkaana-wii-giiwe’aad Program • Ontario Aboriginal Housing ServicesHousing as Healthcare:\n\n\n• OAHS operates on the \"Housing First\" model—recognizing that physical health (diabetes management, wound care) is impossible without a stable home.\n\n\n\nOntario Renovates (OPHI):\n\n\n• Program: Provides forgivable loans (up to $25k) for home repairs focused on Safety & Accessibility.\n\n\n• Health Impact: Installs ramps, grab bars, and accessible showers for seniors/disabled, directly preventing falls and hospitalizations.\n\n\n\nCOVID-19/Isolation Support:\n\n\n• Maintains specific protocols for housing stability during health crises to prevent eviction-related health decline",
    "mental": "Sault Ste. Marie \n\nOAHS partners with local agencies to provide mental health services within residential facilities: \nWillow Haven Supportive Housing: A partnership with CMHA Algoma and Sault Area Hospital that provides 24/7 on-site support. Services include assistance with medical and mental health issues, daily living skills, and addictions support.\n\nYouth Live-In Treatment: OAHS is a core partner in a co-ed live-in treatment home for youth aged 12–16. This model blends western scientific methods with traditional Indigenous approaches, such as individual therapy, group therapy, and sweat lodges \nOur Services \nMkaana-wii-giiwe'aad Program:\n\n\n• Translation: \"Finding Their Way Home\"\n\n\n• Target: Indigenous individuals experiencing homelessness or leaving jail/detox/hospital.\n\n\n• Locations: Sault Ste. Marie, Sudbury, Timmins.\n\n\n• Services: Intensive Case Management that includes \"Warrior Within\" sessions, financial planning, and referrals to addiction treatment.\n\n\n\nSupportive Housing (ISHP):\n\n\n• Model: Funding specifically includes budget for Mental Health & Addiction Workers on-site at housing developments.\n\n\n• Goal: To stop the cycle of \"Eviction due to Mental Health Crisis.\"",
    "spiritual": "Mkaana-wii-giiwe’aad Program • Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services \n\nIndigenous Health \n\nResidents can access dedicated spiritual environments through OAHS partners:Sault area hospital, The Indian Friendship centre.\nCultural Assessments:\n\n\n• Unlike standard housing, OAHS uses a Medicine Wheel assessment tool to determine housing readiness based on spiritual/emotional balance, not just credit scores.\n\n\n\nLand-Based Integration:\n\n\n• Mkaana Program Activities: Explicitly lists Fishing, Smudging, and Visits to Sweat Lodges as part of the housing stabilization plan.\n\n\n• Philosophy: \"Culture is important for healing and identity.\"\n\n\n\nGovernance:\n\n\n• The Board is composed of reps from OFIFC (Friendship Centres), ONWA (Women's Assoc), and MNO (Métis Nation), ensuring all housing policies follow traditional protocols.",
    "emotional": "https://www.ontarioaboriginalhousing.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Mkaana-wii-giiweaad-Program-Brochure.pdf \n\nMkaana-wii-giiwe’aad Program • Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services  \n\nOAHS to create a development that will provide housing to Indigenous women in Sault Ste. Marie",
    "survivors": "211 Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services - Mkaana'aa wii-giiwe'aad Program \n\nhttps://www.ontarioaboriginalhousing.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Mkaana-wii-giiweaad-Program-Brochure.pdf  \n\nOAHS coordinates with the Indian Residential Schools Resolution Health Support Program to ensure survivors and their families receive comprehensive care - Indian Residential Schools Resolution Health Support Program\n\nCommunity and Partner Resources\nSurvivor Gatherings: OAHS partners with community groups like the Children of Shingwauk Alumni Association, which hosts annual events in Sault Ste. Marie dedicated to survivors and intergenerational family members for shared healing and remembrance.\n\nProvincial Funding: New investments for 2025–2026, such as the Indian Residential School Community Engagement Fund (IRSCEF), support community-led, survivor-centric programming and mental health initiatives across Ontario.",
    "youth": "ANCFSAO Releases Indigenous Youth In Transition Resource Guide \n\nMkaana-wii-giiwe’aad Program • Ontario Aboriginal Housing Services \n\nMkaana-wii-giiwe’aad Program: This initiative specifically prioritizes youth (ages 16–24) who are experiencing or at risk of homelessness. It provides housing advocacy, financial planning, and cultural supports to help them find and maintain a home.\n\nYouth in Transition Support: OAHS works alongside the Association of Native Child and Family Service Agencies of Ontario (ANCFSAO), which provides the Pathways to Independence Guide. This resource helps youth aging out of care navigate housing, mental health, and education. \n\n Educational & Financial Assistance\nONPHA Educational Scholarship: Indigenous youth living in OAHS non-profit housing are eligible to apply for scholarships of $2,000 through the Ontario Non-Profit Housing Association. These funds help cover tuition, books, and technology for post-secondary education.\n\nCase Management: Staff assist youth in accessing provincial rent-geared-to-income (RGI) subsidies and other financial resources to ensure long-term housing affordability.",
    "connect": "Research link sources:\nIndian Residential Schools Resolution Health Support Program \n\nIndigenous Friendship Center (Sault Ste. Marie/Elliot Lake)\n\n\nMentorship and Storytelling Programs: Facilitate intergenerational dialogue where Survivors share their stories, knowledge, and resilience with youth, using IFCSSM's cultural programs as a safe space.\n\nCultural Knowledge Transfer: Organize workshops where Survivors teach traditional skills—such as beadwork, drumming, or language—to youth, fostering pride and strengthening cultural identity.\n\nLeverage Existing Programs: Engage with specific IFCSSM programs like Akwe:go, Wasa-Nabin, or Youth Culture Camps to integrate Elders and Survivors into activities.\n\nHealing Circles and Joint Activities: Host joint gatherings that combine healing components with youth-focused activities (e.g., sharing circles, community feasts).\n\nUtilize Support Services: Ensure all gatherings are safe by partnering with Resolution Health Support Workers and Elders, who provide emotional and cultural care during these interactions. \n\nKey Local Resources & Connections:\n\nIndigenous Friendship Centre (Sault Ste. Marie): Provides children, youth, and cultural enrichment programs.\n\nMaamwesying North Shore Tribal Council: Offers Indian Residential School Cultural and Emotional Support services.\n\nHope for Wellness Help Line: 24/7 support for Indigenous peoples, reachable at 1-855-242-3310",
    "population": "",
    "populationNote": "",
